### Why abstract providers behind an interface?

Vendor independence and testability. If your code imports `com.twilio.rest.api.v2010.account.Message` directly, every class is welded to Twilio.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

public interface NotificationProvider {
    DeliveryResult send(Notification notification);
    NotificationChannel channel();
}

public enum NotificationChannel { SMS, EMAIL, PUSH }

public record Notification(String recipient, String message, NotificationChannel channel) {}

public record DeliveryResult(String messageId, DeliveryStatus status, String reason) {}

public enum DeliveryStatus { DELIVERED, FAILED_PERMANENT, FAILED_TRANSIENT }
```

```java
package com.bank.notification;

import java.util.Map;

public class NotificationDispatcher {

    private final Map<NotificationChannel, NotificationProvider> providers;

    public NotificationDispatcher(Map<NotificationChannel, NotificationProvider> providers) {
        this.providers = providers;
    }

    public DeliveryResult dispatch(Notification notification) {
        NotificationProvider provider = providers.get(notification.channel());
        if (provider == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("No provider for " + notification.channel());
        }
        return provider.send(notification);
    }
}
```

```text
Benefits:
  1. Vendor independence: swap Twilio for another SMS vendor -> change one class
  2. Testability: inject FakeProvider in tests -> no real network calls
  3. Failover: route to backup provider when primary is down
```

### How do you classify errors?

Not all failures are equal. Non-retryable failures will never succeed. Retryable failures may succeed shortly.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

public class ErrorClassification {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("NON-RETRYABLE (permanent, never retry):");
        System.out.println("  - Invalid FCM token (device unregistered)");
        System.out.println("  - Hard email bounce (mailbox does not exist)");
        System.out.println("  - Invalid phone number (not a valid MSISDN)");
        System.out.println("  - HTTP 400 (malformed payload)");
        System.out.println("  Action: mark FAILED, clean up bad token, do NOT retry");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("RETRYABLE (transient, retry with backoff):");
        System.out.println("  - Connection timeout");
        System.out.println("  - HTTP 500/502/503 (provider internal error)");
        System.out.println("  - HTTP 429 (rate limited)");
        System.out.println("  Action: retry with exponential backoff + jitter");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
NON-RETRYABLE (permanent, never retry):
  - Invalid FCM token (device unregistered)
  - Hard email bounce (mailbox does not exist)
  - Invalid phone number (not a valid MSISDN)
  - HTTP 400 (malformed payload)
  Action: mark FAILED, clean up bad token, do NOT retry

RETRYABLE (transient, retry with backoff):
  - Connection timeout
  - HTTP 500/502/503 (provider internal error)
  - HTTP 429 (rate limited)
  Action: retry with exponential backoff + jitter
```

### What is a retry storm and how do you prevent it?

If 10,000 messages fail at the same instant and all retry 1 second later, the synchronized wave overwhelms the provider.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

public class RetryStormPrevention {

    public static long calculateDelay(int attempt, long baseMs, long capMs, long jitterMs) {
        long exponential = Math.min(capMs, baseMs * (1L << attempt));
        long jitter = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(0, jitterMs);
        return exponential + jitter;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: retry all 10,000 after exactly 1 second");
        System.out.println("  -> Synchronized wave hammers recovering provider");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: exponential backoff + jitter");
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 4; attempt++) {
            long delay = calculateDelay(attempt, 1000, 30000, 500);
            System.out.printf("  Attempt %d: delay ~%dms%n", attempt + 1, delay);
        }
        System.out.println("  -> Retries spread across a window, not synchronized");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: retry all 10,000 after exactly 1 second
  -> Synchronized wave hammers recovering provider

CORRECT: exponential backoff + jitter
  Attempt 1: delay ~1234ms
  Attempt 2: delay ~2456ms
  Attempt 3: delay ~4123ms
  Attempt 4: delay ~8345ms
  -> Retries spread across a window, not synchronized
```

### How do you handle HTTP 429 specifically?

Honour the Retry-After header. If throttling is sustained, open a per-provider circuit breaker.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

public class Http429Handling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("HTTP 429 handling:");
        System.out.println("  1. Read Retry-After header (e.g. 30 seconds)");
        System.out.println("  2. Wait the specified duration before retrying");
        System.out.println("  3. If sustained 429s: open circuit breaker for this provider");
        System.out.println("     -> Stop sending to exhausted provider");
        System.out.println("     -> Route to backup provider if available");
        System.out.println("  4. Circuit half-opens after cooldown -> test recovery");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
HTTP 429 handling:
  1. Read Retry-After header (e.g. 30 seconds)
  2. Wait the specified duration before retrying
  3. If sustained 429s: open circuit breaker for this provider
  4. Circuit half-opens after cooldown -> test recovery
```

### What happens after max retries?

The message goes to a dead-letter queue (DLQ) for inspection. The DLQ is not a trash can.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

public class DlqHandling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("After max retries (e.g. 5 attempts):");
        System.out.println("  Message -> DLQ (dead-letter queue)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("DLQ is NOT a trash can:");
        System.out.println("  1. Alert if DLQ volume is abnormal");
        System.out.println("  2. Inspect: why did this message fail?");
        System.out.println("  3. Fix the issue (provider config, data)");
        System.out.println("  4. Replay from DLQ after fix");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
After max retries (e.g. 5 attempts):
  Message -> DLQ (dead-letter queue)

DLQ is NOT a trash can:
  1. Alert if DLQ volume is abnormal
  2. Inspect: why did this message fail?
  3. Fix the issue (provider config, data)
  4. Replay from DLQ after fix
```

### How does testability improve with the interface?

In tests, inject a FakeProvider that records what was sent. No real network calls.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class FakeNotificationProvider implements NotificationProvider {

    private final List<Notification> sentNotifications = new ArrayList<>();

    @Override
    public DeliveryResult send(Notification notification) {
        sentNotifications.add(notification);
        return new DeliveryResult("FAKE-001", DeliveryStatus.DELIVERED, "Fake delivery");
    }

    @Override
    public NotificationChannel channel() {
        return NotificationChannel.SMS;
    }

    public List<Notification> getSentNotifications() {
        return sentNotifications;
    }
}
```

```text
Test assertion:
  FakeProvider fake = new FakeProvider();
  dispatcher.dispatch(new Notification("9876543210", "OTP: 123456", SMS));
  assertEquals(1, fake.getSentNotifications().size());
  assertEquals("OTP: 123456", fake.getSentNotifications().get(0).message());
  // No real SMS sent. No Twilio bill.
```

### What is the WRONG pattern vs CORRECT pattern?

```java
package com.bank.notification;

public class WrongVsCorrect {
    // WRONG: retry every failure the same way
    // Priya's mistyped phone -> retried every minute FOREVER
    // Wasting SMS quota on an invalid number

    // CORRECT: classify first
    // Invalid phone -> mark FAILED, clean up token, stop
    // Timeout -> retry with backoff, max 5 attempts -> DLQ

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: retry ALL errors equally");
        System.out.println("  Invalid phone retried 100 times -> wasted quota");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: classify THEN decide");
        System.out.println("  Invalid phone -> FAIL immediately, clean token");
        System.out.println("  Timeout -> retry with backoff -> DLQ after max attempts");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: retry ALL errors equally
  Invalid phone retried 100 times -> wasted quota

CORRECT: classify THEN decide
  Invalid phone -> FAIL immediately, clean token
  Timeout -> retry with backoff -> DLQ after max attempts
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Size provider buckets to real contracts. Monitor bucket depletion. Prefer escalating responses over hard rejects. Combine with per-provider circuit breaker.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Interface per provider (Dependency Inversion + Strategy pattern)
  [x] Classify every error: permanent vs transient
  [x] Permanent: fail fast, clean up bad tokens
  [x] Transient: exponential backoff + jitter (never synchronized)
  [x] HTTP 429: honour Retry-After + circuit breaker for sustained throttling
  [x] DLQ after max retries: alert, inspect, replay
  [x] FakeProvider for unit tests (no real network)
  [x] Per-provider circuit breaker + token bucket for failover
```
